---
id: TE-001
type: rule
family: type-system-evasion
title: Type assertion on an unvalidated boundary value
version: 1

severity: warning
confidence: medium
spectrum_position: 2

targets: [source]
file_extensions: [.ts, .tsx]
allowlist:
  [
    '*.d.ts',
    '**/*.test.ts',
    '**/*.test.tsx',
    '**/*.spec.ts',
    '**/*.spec.tsx',
    '**/__tests__/**',
    '**/__mocks__/**',
    '**/explain/**',
  ]

detection:
  type: regex
  pattern: '(?:JSON\.parse|\.json)\s*\([^)]*\)\s*\)?\s+as\s+[A-Z]'

related: [AP-003, AP-015, GS-002]
enabled: true
opt_in: false
---

`JSON.parse(raw) as Config` and `(await res.json()) as User` do not check
anything. The parse produces a value of unknown shape; the assertion
declares a shape and nothing compares the two. From that line on, the type
system and every reader believe the data has been validated, and the first
missing or misspelled field surfaces as `undefined` somewhere downstream —
in a caller, in a template, in a database write — far from the boundary
that let it through.

This is the exact boundary the family's guidance points at, so parse it for
real. Define the schema once (`z.object({ ... })`), assign the parse result
to an `unknown` local, and hand it to `Schema.parse(raw)` — the throw then
happens here, naming the field that was wrong, instead of surfacing as a
`TypeError` three frames away. If a schema is genuinely too heavy for the
call site, narrow with an explicit type guard that reads the fields you
actually depend on. Either way the check belongs where the data arrives,
because that is the only place an error can still name its source.

## Why It's Harmful

An assertion on a parse result is the most confident thing in the file and
the least justified. `: any` (AP-003) at least announces that nothing is
known; `z.any()` (AP-015) at least ran a schema before opting out. A bare
assertion on `JSON.parse` claims a complete, specific contract — field
names, types, optionality — on evidence that stops at "this was valid
JSON". The family definition's own worked example marks this shape as
*Better, not Best* for precisely that reason: `return data as User` is an
improvement on `any` because it documents intent, and it is still a
fabricated guarantee.

The cost lands on whoever debugs it. A schema failure names the field and
the boundary; an asserted shape fails as a `TypeError` on a property of
`undefined`, one or more call frames away, with a stack that points at the
consumer rather than the malformed input. External JSON is also the input
most likely to drift without warning — an API adds a field, renames one,
starts returning `null` where it used to return a string — and drift in an
asserted shape is invisible until it is an incident.

Scope and known gaps: the detector is line-based and code-scoped (comments
and string literals are masked), and matches a `JSON.parse(...)` or
`.json()` call followed by `as` and a capitalised type name. That
convention is what keeps `as const` out; a lowercase type alias
(`... as config`) is not matched. `as unknown as T` on a parse result is
left to GS-002 rather than double-reported here. A parse split across
lines — `const raw = JSON.parse(text);` then `raw as Config` later — is not
matched either; that flow needs the AST tier. Round-tripping a value your
own code just serialised is the common false positive (hence
`confidence: medium`); suppress it with the reason:
`// @anvil-ignore TE-001 -- round-trip of a value serialised by this module`.
